  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "East Africa has two rainy seasons and long dry spells, so the weather is a real topic of conversation. You’ll also take your first step into the past tense.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Kuna upepo leo.", "base": "It’s windy today." },
      { "target": "Jua kali leo!", "base": "Strong sun today!" },
      { "target": "Msimu gani?", "base": "Which season?" },
      { "target": "Jana mvua ilinyesha.", "base": "Yesterday it rained." },
      { "target": "Ilikuwa baridi sana.", "base": "It was very cold." },
      { "target": "Jana nilikuwa nyumbani.", "base": "Yesterday I was at home." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "seasons",
        "title": "Two rainy seasons",
        "body": [
          "Near the equator the year turns on rain, not on summer and winter. **Masika** are the long rains (about March to May) and **vuli** the short rains (about October to December). The hot, dry months are **kiangazi**.",
          "**Mwezi** means both “month” and “moon”: months were counted by the moon. **Kila mwezi** is “every month”, **kila mwaka** “every year”."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "li-past",
        "title": "The past: -li-",
        "body": [
          "Swap the **-na-** of the present for **-li-** and you are in the past: **inanyesha** (it is raining) becomes **ilinyesha** (it rained), and **ninaenda** (I’m going) becomes **nilienda** (I went).",
          "“To be” works the same way with **-kuwa**: **nilikuwa** (I was), **ilikuwa** (it was), **kulikuwa na** (there was)."
        ],
        "table": [["Now", "Past"], ["ninaenda", "nilienda"], ["inanyesha", "ilinyesha"], ["kuna", "kulikuwa na"]],
        "targetColumns": [0, 1]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Rain is a blessing",
        "body": [
          "A shower at the start of a wedding or a journey is called a good sign. Farmers watch the sky closely: when **masika** comes late, everyone talks about it."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
